WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
Teaching a scheduler, and watching it cheat.

2
00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:13.700
The agent only sees what a real tutor could: the streak of correct answers and the last grade. Its action is how long to wait.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:22.367
The reward: waited fourteen days and still remembered? You proved fourteen days of memory. Forgetting costs double, and every review costs effort.

4
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:34.967
Same Q-learning as the gridworld. At first it guesses badly and students forget. Within about fifteen thousand students, the return climbs and settles. Five seconds on a laptop.

5
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:46.400
Fixed schedules trade effort for recall along a curve. Every day is near perfect, but costs sixty reviews. Leitner sits at ninety-three percent with ten.

6
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:55.467
The learned scheduler lands top-left: ninety-seven percent recall with under twelve reviews. Same recall as every two days, with sixty percent less effort.

7
00:00:55.967 --> 00:01:05.033
Now change the reward. Plus one per correct answer sounds right, but the agent quizzes you almost every day: easy points, huge workload.

8
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:11.033
Reward long gaps instead, and it simply never quizzes you. Recall collapses to twenty-two percent.

9
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:18.167
Reinforcement learning optimises exactly what you reward, not what you meant. Designing the reward is the real work.

